  "detail": "Thank you for the shared `gateProvenance` check; the approach is right. Rounds 3\u20135 never reached this finding: each implement session failed at startup with a Claude login error, so the code is unchanged since round 2.\n\nThe working-tree comparison trusts the checkout's own index. An index entry marked `assume-unchanged` or `skip-worktree` is skipped by both `git update-index --refresh` and `git diff --name-only <sha> --`. Review reproduced this in a temporary repository: after `git update-index --assume-unchanged tracked.txt` (or `--skip-worktree`) and a rewrite of tracked.txt, the check returned `paths: []` and `trackedClean: true` while the file held different bytes. An ordinary rewrite was correctly reported. Gate confinement normally prevents a gate from writing the index, but this check must not depend on that.\n\nFix: for the working-tree comparison, build a fresh temporary index from the checked commit and compare the checkout against it.\n- Put the index file in a private temporary directory outside the checkout, and remove it afterwards.\n- Run `worktreeGit` with `GIT_INDEX_FILE` pointing at it: `git read-tree <checkedSha>`, then `git update-index --refresh` (exit 0 or 1), then `git diff --name-only -z --no-renames`. The checked commit's tree carries no flags.\n- Follow the existing temporary-index pattern in src/git/repos.ts (around lines 505\u2013530 and 1235). src/git/hardening.ts already lets `GIT_INDEX_FILE` through.\n- Keep the separate `--cached` comparison of the real index against the checked commit.\n- Keep failing closed (`trackedClean: false` with a reason) if any step fails.\n\nTests, with real git: a passing gate that sets `assume-unchanged` and rewrites a tracked file records no evidence; the same with `skip-worktree`; the ordinary rewrite and the untracked/ignored-only cases keep their current results. Each new test fails without the fix. Run only the test files you change plus test/gate-executor.test.ts, `bunx biome check` on changed files and `bunx tsc --noEmit`. Never run the full suite in a factory run."
